---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def, { TOPSOIL_MATERIALS } from '../calculators/topsoil';
import { bulkResult, coverageFt2PerYd3 } from '../lib/bulk';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const ex = bulkResult((20 * 10 * 4) / 12, 10, 1.1, [0.75, 1]);
const bed = bulkResult(8 * 4 * 1, 0, 1.1, [0.75, 1]);
const coverage = [1, 2, 4, 6, 12].map((d) => ({ d, yd: coverageFt2PerYd3(d), ton: coverageFt2PerYd3(d) / 1.1 }));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="topsoil-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Topsoil Calculator: Cubic Yards, Tons & Bags for Lawns and Beds"
  description="Free topsoil calculator. Enter the area and depth to get cubic yards, tons, and bags of topsoil or compost for a new lawn, garden bed, or raised bed."
  h1="Topsoil Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Calculate the cubic yards, tons, or bags of topsoil you need for a new lawn, a garden bed, or raised beds, with extra
    for settling.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate topsoil</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Measure the area's length and width in feet and multiply for square feet.</li>
    <li>Multiply by the depth in feet (4 inches = 0.333 ft) for cubic feet.</li>
    <li>Divide by 27 for cubic yards. Multiply by about 1.1 for tons of dry screened topsoil.</li>
    <li>Add 10–20%, because loose soil settles once it is watered and walked on.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: a 20 × 10 ft lawn area, 4 inches deep</h2>
  <p>
    20 × 10 × 0.333 = 66.7 cubic feet, or 2.47 cubic yards. With 10% extra that is {fmt(ex.cubicYards)} cubic yards,
    about {fmt(ex.tons)} tons. Order {fmt(ex.orderYards)} yards.
  </p>

  <h2 id="raised-bed">Example: filling an 8 × 4 ft raised bed, 12 inches deep</h2>
  <p>
    8 × 4 × 1 = 32 cubic feet, or {fmt(bed.cubicYards)} cubic yards. That is {bed.bags[0].count} bags of 0.75 cubic feet
    or {bed.bags[1].count} bags of 1 cubic foot. For more than one bed, a bulk yard of soil or a garden mix is usually
    cheaper than bags.
  </p>

  <h2 id="weight">How much does a yard of topsoil weigh?</h2>
  <p>
    A cubic yard of dry, screened topsoil weighs about <strong>1 to 1.2 tons</strong>. Wet soil can top 1.3 tons, while
    compost is far lighter. Weight matters when you haul it yourself: one cubic yard can exceed a small pickup's payload.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th class="num">Typical tons per cubic yard</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{TOPSOIL_MATERIALS.map((m) => <tr><td>{m.label}</td><td class="num">{m.tonsPerYd3}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="coverage">How much area does a yard of topsoil cover?</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Depth</th><th class="num">Sq ft per cubic yard</th><th class="num">Sq ft per ton (1.1 t/yd³)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{coverage.map((c) => <tr><td>{c.d} in</td><td class="num">{fmt(c.yd, 0)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(c.ton, 0)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="depth">How much topsoil do you need for a lawn or garden?</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>Typical depth</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>New lawn from seed or sod</td><td>4–6 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Top dressing an existing lawn</td><td>¼–½ in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Flower and shrub beds</td><td>6–8 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Vegetable beds</td><td>8–12 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Raised beds</td><td>The full depth of the bed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Laying a new lawn? Once the soil is down, the <a href="/sod-calculator/">sod calculator</a> gives you pallets and
    pieces. For beds, finish with 2–3 inches from the <a href="/mulch-calculator/">mulch calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Seeding instead of laying sod? <a href="/grass-seed-calculator/">Work out how much grass seed to spread</a> once the soil is down.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How much extra topsoil should I order?</h3>
  <p>
    Plan on 10–20% more than the measured volume. Delivered soil is loose and fluffy; it settles as it is raked, rolled,
    and watered.
  </p>
  <h3>What is the difference between topsoil and garden soil?</h3>
  <p>
    Topsoil is screened native soil, used to build up grade and for lawns. Garden soil is topsoil blended with compost or
    other organic matter for planting beds. Both are sold by the cubic yard or bag and calculate the same way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Volume = area × depth, converted to cubic yards (27 cubic feet per yard).</li>
    <li>Weight uses typical densities in US tons; soil moisture changes the real number.</li>
    <li>Bulk orders round up to the next half yard or half ton; bag counts always round up.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
